## What the supplied material establishes

- `pilot.py`: fixed seed `random.Random(20261019)`; draws (x, y) uniform on [0,1)^2 in batches of 100 000,
  counts x²+y² ≤ 1; estimate = 4·inside/total. Runs for 540 s of wall-clock time (so the sample count
  depends on CPU speed and host contention, not fixed in advance). Prints a `HUTIAN_WAKE:` interim
  estimate after ~60 s. Records a trace point (elapsed s, cumulative samples, running estimate) about
  every 10 s. Writes JSON `{samples, estimate, ci95, seconds, trace}`.
- CI convention in the script: normal-approximation (Wald) binomial interval,
  `4·p̂ ± 1.96·4·sqrt(p̂(1−p̂)/n)`. With n ~ 10⁹ the normal approximation is excellent; the analysis
  recomputes it independently and cross-checks it with the Wilson score interval.
- `extended.py MINUTES OUT`: seed `int(MINUTES·1000)`, same sampler, runs MINUTES minutes, prints every
  5 min, writes `{minutes, samples, estimate, ci95}`.
- The running-estimate trace points are cumulative (nested samples), so they are strongly correlated;
  the convention for the figure is the running estimate with the true value pi as a horizontal line,
  optionally the ±1.96σ(n) envelope around pi, samples on a log x-axis.

